<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- macOS shows these strings verbatim in its own permission prompt, which
         makes them the most-read copy in the product: every user meets them the
         first time the assistant touches one of these folders, inside a system
         dialog that offers Allow and Don't Allow and no way to ask a question.

         So they say what the assistant will do with the folder, in the words a
         non-developer uses for it. They said "to open projects located there"
         until the day before release — accurate about a code editor, and about
         nothing this product does now. Someone deciding whether to let an app
         read their Documents folder should not have to work out what a project
         is, and a prompt that reads as jargon is a prompt people deny.

         Same rule as the rest of the user-facing copy (see CLAUDE.md): "the
         agent", never "prime-agent", and never a developer's word for the
         user's own files. -->

    <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LAF Agent reads and organizes files on your Desktop when you ask it to.</string>

    <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LAF Agent reads and organizes files in your Documents folder when you ask it to.</string>

    <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LAF Agent reads and organizes files in your Downloads folder when you ask it to.</string>

    <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LAF Agent reads and organizes files on connected drives when you ask it to.</string>

    <key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LAF Agent reads and organizes files on shared network drives when you ask it to.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
